module

public import Sheaf.RingTheory.PolynomialModuleGoodLocus
import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Basis

/-!
# Localization of the polynomial-module good locus

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 051X (lemma-generic-flatness-locus-localize),
with equation-flat-and-finitely-presented and equation-good-locus, specialized to
polynomial algebras (blueprint: lem-sfb-polynomial-good-locus-localization).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

namespace MvPolynomial

/-- Contracting primes identifies the good locus of the localized polynomial module
with the intersection of the original good locus and the principal open of the localizer.
The action on the fraction module is the canonical action `localizedPolynomialModule`:
`X i` sends `m / h^a` to `(X i • m) / h^a`.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 051X (lemma-generic-flatness-locus-localize),
polynomial specialization (blueprint: lem-sfb-polynomial-good-locus-localization).

Proof:
1. The polynomial universal property and the ring-localization universal property
(`lem-weil-pbc-ring-localization-universal-property`) identify the localization of
`A[x]` at the constants generated by `t` with `A_t[x]`. Both fraction modules have
the relation `t^k (t^b m - t^a m') = 0`; the comparison respects every variable action.
2. Goodness persists under further principal localization. The tensor comparison
`lem-weil-pbc-localization-module-tensor` and
`lem-weil-pbc-free-module-base-change-free` give a localized basis, of arbitrary rank.
Apply `lem-trf-localization-preserves-finite-presentation` to the polynomial module
at the powers of the constant `t`, and use step 1 for its coefficient localization.
3. For `g = a / h^r` in `R_h`, the ring-localization universal property identifies
`(R_h)_g` with `R_(a*h)`: each makes both `a` and `h` invertible. The module universal
property identifies the corresponding fraction modules, compatibly with variables.
The forward map sends `(m/h^s)/g^k` to `h^(r*k) h^(-s) a^(-k) m` in `M_(a*h)`;
the reverse sends `m/(a*h)^k` to `(m/h^k)/(a/1)^k`. The composites fix `M` and
are identities by uniqueness. The coefficient ring isomorphisms extend to polynomials.
4. For a prime `q` in the localized good locus choose a good `g = a/h^r` outside `q`.
Its contraction `p` avoids `h` and `a`, hence `a*h`. Step 3 transports a basis and a
finite presentation to `M_(a*h)`, so `a*h` witnesses membership in the original locus.
5. Conversely a prime `p` in `D(h)` and the original good locus has a good witness
`a` outside `p`. Step 2 localizes the good module `M_a` further at `h/1`, and step 3
identifies it with `(M_h)_(a/1)`. The prime over `p` avoids `a/1`, proving membership
in the localized locus. The two inclusions give the asserted image equality.
-/
theorem polynomialModuleGoodLocus_localization
    (R : Type u) [CommRing R] (n : ℕ) (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M]
    [Module (MvPolynomial (Fin n) R) M] (h : R) :
    PrimeSpectrum.comap (algebraMap R (Localization.Away h)) ''
      (@polynomialModuleGoodLocus (Localization.Away h) _ n
        (LocalizedModule ((Submonoid.powers h).map (C (σ := Fin n))) M) _
        (localizedPolynomialModule R n M h)) =
      (PrimeSpectrum.basicOpen h : Set (PrimeSpectrum R)) ∩ polynomialModuleGoodLocus R n M := by
  sorry

end MvPolynomial
